MOFs in the solid state, the transformations of which involve cooperative bond breaking and formation rather than the more frequently encountered counterion-exchange mechanism.3,14,15b
Interest in the coordination chemistry of $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}$ has been driven by both its flexibility and its varied mode of coordination with TM ions.17 The reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}$ with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ under different conditions affords seven isomers based on different $Z\mathrm{n}$ clusters that act as secondary building units (SBUs).18 Diverse ligand geometries such as tetrahedral, irregular, or near-flattened are observed in these solids, and this flexibility arises from twisting of the benzoate moieties around the central quaternary carbon atom (denoted as $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{core}}$ in Figure 1A) through ether links. With this in mind, we sought to explore the potential for constructing stimuli-responsive flexible MOFs from $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}$ . A series of isostructural anionic MOFs with the general formula $\left[\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}\right]^{2-}\cdot2[\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}]^{+}$ ·8DMA $\mathbf{\tilde{M}=C\bar{o}}$ , Mn, Cd; DMA ${\mathbf\xi}=N,N.$ -dimethylacetamide) were recently reported by Cao and co-workers.19 These MOFs crystallize in the triclinic space group $P\overline{{1}}_{\cdot}$ ̅, and the asymmetric unit comprises one $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ ligand, two crystallographically independent $\mathrm{TM}^{2+}$ ions, and a $\mathrm{[NH}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}]^{+}$ counterion generated in situ under solvothermal conditions. One of the $\bar{\mathrm{TM}}^{2+}$ ions is in a general position, while the other resides on an inversion center with $50\%$ site occupancy. In our hands, two MOFs with the general anionic framework formula $[\mathrm{Mn}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]^{2-}$ (1) were obtained under solvothermal conditions from differing ratios of $^{N,N}$ - dimethylformamide (DMF) and water: $\lbrack\mathrm{Mn}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]^{2-}{\cdot}2\cdot$ - $\big[\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\big)_{2}\big]^{+}$ ·9DMF (1a) and $\left[\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\left(\mathrm{L}\right)_{2}\right]^{2-}\cdot2.$ - $\mathrm{[H}_{3}\mathrm{O}]^{+}$ ·12DMF (1b) [for experimental details, see the Supporting Information (SI)]. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) studies revealed that both structures display the same connectivity between the $\mathbf{M}{\mathbf{n}_{3}}$ SBUs and $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ linkers, resulting in anionic 3D frameworks with the same topology but disparate counterions (Figure S1 in the SI). Although this is the case, different conformations of $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ in the solids results in varied dimensionality in 1a and 1b; the unit cell volume of 1a $\left(V=2671\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\right)$ ) is $14\%$ smaller than that of 1b $\left(V=3052\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\right)$ ). Comparison of the conformations of $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ in 1a and 1b clearly shows that along the a axis, two of the four arms of $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ are more splayed in 1b than in 1a, causing the a axis of 1b to be 1.7 Å longer (Figure S2). Thus, the anionic framework of 1b can be regarded as an “expanded” form of 1a. The flexibility of $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ within these anionic MOFs prompted us to investigate the possibility of exchanging the $\bar{{\bf M}\bf n}^{2+}$ or $\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}^{+}/\mathrm{H}_{3}^{-}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ ions with other $\mathrm{TM}^{2+}$ ions such as ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}.$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ .
A colorless single crystal of 1a was fixed to the top of a glass fiber and immersed in a $0.2\mathrm{~M~}$ DMF solution of cobalt(II) nitrate. The crystal did not dissolve but turned to light-purple color within a few hours, indicating penetration of $\mathrm{\bar{C}}0^{\hat{2}+}$ into the framework. After $24\ \mathrm{h},$ a deep-purple crystal of 2 was obtained and analyzed crystallographically. Surprisingly, the crystal structure of 2 is significantly different from that of 1a, despite the fact that they have the same space group and similar unit cell parameters. The asymmetric unit of 2 comprises one $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ linker, one aqua ligand, and three crystallographically independent ${{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}}/{{\mathrm{\bar{C}}}{\mathrm{o}}^{2+}}$ ions; two of these reside on inversion centers with $50\%$ site occupancy while the third occupies a general position. In comparison with 1a, the SBU in 2 becomes neutral by uptake of one $\mathrm{TM}^{2+}$ (with $50\%$ site occupancy) per asymmetric unit. On the basis of elemental analysis (EA), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) studies, the formula of 2 was found to be $\mathrm{MnCo}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ ·12DMF. This implies that in addition to insertion of additional ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ into the framework, partial exchange of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ for ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ also occurs. Such an insertion/ exchange process, proceeding via an $S C{\mathrm{-}}S C$ transformation, must therefore involve the cooperative breakage/formation of metal−carboxylate bonds. This is evidenced by the fact that in moving from 1a to 2, two of the four carboxylate groups in the $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ ligand change their coordination modes (Figure S3). To provide a better understanding of the mechanism of the $S C-$ SC transformation, we carefully compared the structural features of these two MOFs. Figure 2 shows extended structures of 1a and 2 along the $b$ axis. In 1a, the $\mathbf{M}{\mathbf{n}_{3}}$ SBUs align along the $\mathbf{\Psi}_{c}$ axis and are mutually interconnected by $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ linkers with a short distance $(7.0\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ between two nearest neighboring clusters. Insertion of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions in the synthesis of 2 results in linkage of discrete $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}$ clusters in 1a to afford an infinite MnCo chain with a zigzag arrangement along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ axis.
One octahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ (Co1 and its symmetry equivalents) resides on an inversion center (between the positions of two neighboring $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}$ SBUs in 1a) and is bonded by four carboxylate oxygen atoms from different $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ linkers and two oxygen atoms from aquo ligands. We hypothesize that in order to connect the inserted $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\left(\mathrm{Col}\right)$ and meet the constraints of octahedral geometry, the carboxylate groups coordinated to two nearby terminal $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ ions (Mn2 and Mn2A) of the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}$ SBUs in 1a must change their coordination modes with a high degree of cooperativity: the chelating carboxylate groups change to bridging mode, the chelating/bridging carboxylate groups change to $\mu_{3}–\eta^{2}{:}\eta^{1}$ -bridging mode, and the bridging carboxylate groups remain the same (Figure 2). This would mean that the terminal octahedral $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ ions of the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}$ SBUs in 1a would become four-coordinate with tetrahedral geometry prior to displacement by the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ( $\mathrm{\dot{C}}\circ2$ and Co2A) ions. This theory is based on the short $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co-O}}$ bond lengths evident in 2 (four $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}2-{\mathrm{O}}$ bonds in the range $1.950{\scriptstyle-2.043}\mathrm{~\AA})$ . The variation in the coordination mode of the carboxylate groups is directed by the rotation around $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{core}}$ within the $\mathrm{L}^{\bar{4-}}$ linker, resulting in a slight change in ligand conformation and an expansion of the entire framework by $6.5\%$ in going from 1a to 2. Single crystals of 1b are also transformed into 2 via a similar $S C{\mathrm{-}}S C$ process when treated in an identical fashion, and in this case, the framework contracts by $7.5\%$ , demonstrating that the $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ linker can adjust its conformation to allow for either expansion or contraction of frameworks of 1.
The insertion of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions into the anionic framework of 1 indicated that other dications such as $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ may be accommodated, given the degree of ligand versatility observed. Crystals of 1a were found to capture ${\bar{\mathrm{Cu}}}^{2+}$ from a DMF solution via a process similar to that for ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ capture. An intermediate structure of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -inserted crystals $\left(\mathbf{1a-Cu}\right)$ was successfully solved and found to be isostructural to that of the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ -inserted analogue (Figure S4), but these crystals persistently lost their monocrystallinity following completion of the insertion/exchange process over a $24\bar{\mathrm{~h~}}$ period. The insertion of either $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ or $Z\bar{\bf n}^{2+}$ into 1a resulted in crystal cracking/degradation and loss of single-crystallinity, thus preventing further structural analysis. Notably, crystals of 1a captured $\bar{\bf N i}^{2+}$ ions via an $S C{\mathrm{-}}S C$ process that is significantly different from that for either ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ or ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ . Light-green crystals of 3 were obtained when 1a or 1b was soaked in a $0.2{\bf M}$ DMF solution of nickel(II) nitrate for $24\mathrm{h}.$ . SCXRD analysis revealed that 3 retains the same space group $(P\overline{{1}})$ but has a much larger unit cell volume $(V=35\bar{4}1\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ than 1a. The asymmetric unit of 3 comprises one $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ linker, three and a half aquo ligands, half a ligated DMF, and three crystallographically independent $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}/\$ $\bar{\bf N i}^{2+}$ ions, implying the insertion of $\bar{\bf N i}^{2+}$ into the 1a framework. EA, TGA, and EDX studies indicated the formula of 3 to be $\mathrm{Mn_{1.3}N i_{2.7}L_{2}(H_{2}O)_{7}(D M F){\cdot}16D M F}.$ Comparison of the structural features of 1a and 3 indicates how the $S C{\mathrm{-}}S C$ transformation proceeds. Figure 3 shows views of 1a and 3 along the $b$ axis. The 3D framework of 1a can be viewed as discrete 2D layers interconnected by the carboxylate groups coordinated to the terminal $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ cations (Mn2 and Mn2A) of the $\mathbf{M}{\mathbf{n}_{3}}$ SBUs in a chelating coordination mode. The distance between neighboring layers is 11.664 Å. We hypothesize that the chelating carboxylate groups in 1a are cleaved from the terminal $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ cations and that two proximal (now unbound) carboxylate groups coordinate to an inserted $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ cation (Ni1). These would be located at apical positions, with ligated solvent molecules occupying the equatorial plane of the resulting octahedral geometry. In addition, each terminal $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ of the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}$ SBU (with $85\%$ probability) in 1a is displaced by a $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ion, with two aquo ligands replacing the cleaved chelating carboxylate group to maintain an octahedral geometry. The overall result of this process would be the trimetallic NiMnNi cluster as the SBU of 3. It is noted that to accommodate the insertion of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ between the 2D layers of 1a, these layers are pushed ${\sim}5$ Å further apart in 3, a feature directed by a conformational change in the $\mathrm{L}^{4-}$ linker. As a result, the a axis of 3 is significantly longer than that of 1a, with an overall expansion of $33\%$ upon $S C{\mathrm{-}}S C$ transformation. It is worth mentioning that attempts to synthesize 2 and 3 by conventional means (by combining mixed metal salts) failed, indicating that cation-insertion-induced SC−SC transformations represent a feasible method for the generation of new coordination compounds from anionic MOFs constructed with flexible ligands.
Photometric experiments were conducted to establish the cation uptake capabilities and kinetics of bulk 1a over a $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ period (Figure S5). ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ was used as a suitable probe for uptake experiments because of its stability and UV spectroscopic profile. Framework 1a was introduced into a $0.2{\bf M}$ DMF solution of cobalt(II) nitrate, and the concentration was measured as a function of time; the observed ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ concentration was found to decrease by $19\%$ over 8.5 and $37\%$ over $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . No significant decrease in ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ concentration was detected after $24\ \mathrm{h},$ indicating uptake completion. The overall ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ uptake capacity of 1a was found to be $3.12~\mathrm{mol}$ $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ $(93\mathrm{mg}\bar{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ , which correlates with the results of SCXRD and EDX analysis. Additional EDX studies were performed to evaluate the selective uptake/exchange capabilities of 1a toward a series of divalent metal ions $\mathrm{\left(Zn^{2+}\times\bar{C u}^{2+}>C o^{2+}>N i^{2+}\gg\right.}$ $\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}>\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}>\mathrm{Sr^{2+}})$ . It was further demonstrated that crystals of 1a selectively capture ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ from a DMF solution containing equimolar amounts of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}.$ , implying that 1a could be used to sequester divalent cations from a mixture (Table S1 in the SI). Interestingly, 1a shows highly selective uptake of $\mathrm{TM}^{2+}$ ions in the presence of multiple competing alkali-metal cations such as $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and ${{\bf{N}}_{\bf{a}}}^{+}$ . Powder XRD (PXRD) and inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) studies of crystalline 1a immersed in a DMF solution of either lithium or sodium nitrate showed that the anionic framework of 1a remained unchanged but that the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}^{+}$ counterions were fully replaced by $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ or ${{\bf{N}}_{}}{{\bf{a}}_{}}^{+}$ ions. Subsequent competition reactions between combinations of either cobalt(II) nitrate or nickel(II) nitrate and lithium nitrate or sodium nitrate followed by ICP-OES and EDX measurements indicated that either ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ or $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions were preferentially sequestered by 1a, with only trace amounts of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ or ${{\bf{N}}_{}}{{\bf{a}}_{}}^{+}$ detected (Table S2). The soft framework of 1a shows a strong preference for $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{\DeltaNi}^{2+}$ via SC−SC processes, and this may be explained by the significantly stronger interaction of carboxylate groups with the $\mathrm{TM}^{2+}$ cations.
The ability to interchange and control the metal composition in such systems has the clear potential to vary the magnetic properties of the resulting solid.4 Magnetic susceptibility measurements on powdered microcrystalline samples of complexes $_{1-3}$ were carried out in an applied field of $0.1\mathrm{~T~}$ , and the data are plotted as the $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ product versus temperature (Figure S6). For all of the compounds, the data are somewhat similar: $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ decreases slowly with decreasing temperature, indicative of relatively weak antiferromagnetic interactions between neighboring metal ions. The plots of $\chi^{-1}$ versus $T$ (not shown) were linear, permitting fits to the Curie−Weiss law, $\chi^{-1}=\big(T-\Theta\big)/C,$ which afforded Weiss constants $\left(\Theta\right)$ of −14.4 K (1a), $-10.5\mathrm{~K~}$ (2), and $-9.30\mathrm{~K~}$ (3). For complex 1, the susceptibility data could be fitted to the simple isotropic Hamiltonian $\hat{H}\stackrel{\leftarrow}{=}-2J(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{Mn1}}{\cdot}\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{Mn2}}+\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{Mn2}}{\cdot}\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{Mn3}})$ , which describes the system as a series of magnetically isolated $\mathrm{Mn}1{-}\mathrm{Mn}2{-}\mathrm{Mn}3$ linear trimers in which all of the $\mathbf{M}^{\mathbf{\hdots}}\mathbf{M}$ interactions are equivalent. The upper solid red line in Figure S6 is the fit with $J_{\mathrm{Mn-Mn}}^{\mathrm{-}}=-1.42\mathrm{cm}^{\mathrm{-1}}$ and $g=2.00$ . The data for complex 3 could be treated in a similar fashion by assuming isolated linear $\mathrm{Ni}1{-}\mathrm{Mn}1{-}\mathrm{Ni}2$ chains $\begin{array}{r}{\big[\hat{H}=-2J(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{Nil}}^{\prime}{\cdot}\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{Mnl}}+\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{Mnl}}{\cdot}\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\mathrm{Ni2}})\big]}\end{array}$ and noninteracting (paramagnetic) Ni monomers. The lower solid red line in Figure S6 is the fit with $J_{\mathrm{Ni-Mn}}=-1.43~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $g=$ 2.21. The magnetochemical analysis of ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ -based complexes is nontrivial because of the combined effects of spin−orbit coupling and the distortion of the octahedral crystal field. This and the presence of significant crystallographic disorder precluded any meaningful quantitative analysis of the magnetic data for complex 2.
In summary, we have demonstrated that soft anionic MOFs (1a and 1b) are capable of sequestering metal cations through cooperative $S C{\mathrm{-}}S C$ processes, resulting in novel neutral and heterobimetallic systems with rearranged framework structures. These $S C{\mathrm{-}}S C$ transformations are directed by adaptation of the conformation and coordination modes of the flexible organic carboxylate linker $\left(\mathrm{L}^{4-}\right)$ to meet the coordination environment of inserted/exchanged metal ions, a process involving cooperative breakage/formation of metal−carboxylate coordination bonds in the solid state. These anionic MOFs also exhibit exceptional uptake/exchange selectivity for $\mathrm{TM}^{2+}$ ions in the presence of competing alkali-metal cations. In stark contrast to the rigid open 3D frameworks of zeolites, which tend to be less affected by ion exchange, the framework flexibility observed for the soft anionic MOFs reported here holds great potential for the design of selective sensors, selective ion-exchange media, and materials for the removal of toxic heavy-metal ions, all of which will be the focus of future studies.20
